class.description:
[SerializerOptions] holds the options of [method ResourceTextSerializer.serialize], [method ResourceTextSerializer.deserialize], [method ResourceTextSerializer.resource_to_dictionary] and [method TOML.serialize]. It is a [Resource], so a set of options can be edited in the Inspector and saved as a preset ([code].tres[/code]) that gives the same output when loaded again.
[code]SerializerOptions.new()[/code] has the class defaults, which are the same as the defaults of the project settings under [code]filesystem/resources/text_formats/[/code]. Passing [code]null[/code] instead uses the current values of these project settings, like [ResourceSaver] and [ResourceLoader] do. Options that a format does not use are ignored.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var options = SerializerOptions.new()
options.indent = "\t"
options.sort_keys = true
var text = ResourceTextSerializer.serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.FORMAT_JSON, options)

# A preset made in the Inspector:
var preset = load("res://export_options.tres") as SerializerOptions
var xml = ResourceTextSerializer.serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.FORMAT_XML, preset)
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var options = new SerializerOptions { Indent = "\t", SortKeys = true };
string text = ResourceTextSerializer.Serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.Format.Json, options);

// A preset made in the Inspector:
var preset = GD.Load<SerializerOptions>("res://export_options.tres");
string xml = ResourceTextSerializer.Serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.Format.Xml, preset);
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
Formats with options of their own extend this class (for example [code]class_name MyFormatOptions extends SerializerOptions[/code], or a C++ subclass in a module) instead of passing untyped values.
